## 5. Employee Salary — Display Employees Earning Above a Salary

Question: Write a LINQ program to display salaries greater than a user-entered amount.

```
using System;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter number of employees: ");
        int n = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());

        double[] salary = new double[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            Console.Write("Enter salary: ");
            salary[i] = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
        }

        Console.Write("Enter salary limit: ");
        double limit = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());

        var result = from s in salary
                     where s > limit
                     select s;

        Console.WriteLine("Salaries above the limit:");

        foreach (double s in result)
            Console.WriteLine(s);

        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}
```

# Part 2: Lambda Expressions — 5 college practical programs

A lambda expression uses the `=>` operator. It is commonly used with LINQ to filter or process records.

## 1. Student Marks — Display Passed Students' Marks

Question: Write a C# program using a lambda expression to display marks greater than or equal to 50.

```
using System;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter number of students: ");
        int n = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());

        int[] marks = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            Console.Write("Enter marks: ");
            marks[i] = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());
        }

        var result = marks.Where(x => x >= 50);

        foreach (int m in result)
            Console.WriteLine(m);

        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}
```

Important line: `marks.Where(x => x >= 50)`

